    "cave": {
      "word": "CAVE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A hollow place in the earth, either natural or artificial; a subterraneous cavity; a cavern; a den.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Any hollow place, or part; a cavity. [Obs.] \"The cave of the ear.\" Bacon. Cave bear (Zoöl.), a very large fossil bear (Ursus spelæus) similar to the grizzly bear, but large; common in European caves. was a cave. Tylor. caves, now usually regarded as a large variety of the living African spotted hyena. believed to be a large variety of the African lion.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To make hollow; to scoop out. [Obs.] The mouldred earth cav'd the banke. Spenser.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To dwell in a cave. [Obs.] Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "Etym: [See To cave in, below.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To fall in or down; as, the sand bank caved. Hence (Slang), to retreat from a position; to give way; to yield in a disputed matter. To cave in. Etym: [Flem. inkalven.] (a) To fall in and leave a hollow, as earth on the side of a well or pit. (b) To submit; to yield. [Slang] H. Kingsley.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
